      "problem": "Compute $P(X\\le -5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\le -3, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -5) & =P(-\\infty <X\\le -5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{3}{x}]}_{-\\infty }^{-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
